{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on Popper's terms as a 'critical rationalist' decision rule that maximizes informational content without assigning probability, but it costs the agent any guarantee of practical success or future reliability.","counterconditions":["If the agent requires a probabilistic guarantee of safety (e.g., in bridge building) that content-maximization cannot provide.","If 'acting on' a theory logically entails an expectation of its future validity, reintroducing induction.","If the 'severity' of tests itself relies on inductive assumptions about the uniformity of nature."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Adopt a decision rule where 'preference' means 'selection for testing and action based on highest surviving empirical content,' explicitly decoupling selection from expectation of truth. The mechanism is a non-inductive maximization of information: we choose the theory that tells us the most about the world because it has survived the most severe attempts to falsify it, not because we believe it will continue to hold.","optional_refs":["98effe7a8569cdf52812948906e55ba5ea5ead7c155fa8ea6439b3bb444bdbb4","108690f4d483ff5fd93b6328a7481e23b0347da3519e52acc7b145634ed90465"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.85},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference smuggles induction back in because any 'action' based on corroboration implicitly assumes that the future will resemble the past (the Principle of Uniformity), which Popper explicitly rejected as a justification for scientific inference.","counterconditions":["If Popper can define 'rational action' in a way that does not involve prediction.","If the 'principle of uniformity' is not required for specific engineering decisions but only for universal laws.","If the preference is viewed as a convention rather than an epistemic claim."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Analyze the pragmatic structure of 'acting on' a theory. To build a bridge or administer a vaccine is to predict a future state. If corroboration (past survival) is the sole basis for this prediction, then the agent is effectively assuming that past performance predicts future results. This is the definition of induction. Popper's denial of this link creates a gap between his epistemology and his praxis that cannot be bridged without covertly accepting uniformity.","optional_refs":["9da4686436c6061c2ac9acd5de24848506d3594987582746916a72a1fb772cd3"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.75},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The dilemma is dissolved by reinterpreting 'preference' as a purely negative heuristic: we do not prefer the corroborated theory because it is good, but only because all known alternatives are demonstrably worse (falsified).","counterconditions":["If the set of possible theories is infinite, eliminating some does not make the survivor 'better' in any actionable sense.","If 'acting' requires a positive expectation of success, not just the absence of known failure.","If the criteria for 'severe testing' implicitly assume inductive regularities."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Shift the locus of value from the survivor to the eliminated rivals. The mechanism is 'survival of the fittest' in a strictly negative sense: the preferred theory is simply the one that has not yet been killed. Action is taken on it not because it is expected to work, but because there is no known reason (from current tests) why it should fail. This avoids induction by refusing to posit any positive attribute (probability, truth, reliability) to the survivor.","optional_refs":["108690f4d483ff5fd93b6328a7481e23b0347da3519e52acc7b145634ed90465"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.65},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Popper's position is sustainable only if we accept a 'propensity' interpretation of probability, where corroboration reflects the objective tendency of a theory to withstand tests, thereby allowing a non-inductive basis for preference.","counterconditions":["If propensities are themselves unobservable and metaphysically dubious.","If measuring propensity still requires assuming the future resembles the past.","If Popper's own writings on propensity are inconsistent with his earlier rejection of probability."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Introduce Popper's propensity theory as the missing link. The mechanism posits that theories have an objective 'propensity' to survive tests based on their fit with reality. Corroboration is the measure of this propensity. Preferring the better-corroborated theory is thus preferring the one with the higher objective propensity to be true (or close to it), which is a factual claim about the world, not an inductive inference from past to future.","optional_refs":["d8e19aad077566587af2a5f1022e998eae58fb81657b5797a6283b595ddd1cdc"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.45},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference is a 'conventionalist' move disguised as critical rationalism: scientists agree to treat the best-corroborated theory as 'true for now' by social decree, acknowledging this is a pragmatic fiction necessary for coordination, not an epistemic truth.","counterconditions":["If this concession admits that Popper's epistemology cannot guide action on its own.","If the convention itself rests on an inductive hope that the fiction usually works.","If individual scientists genuinely believe in the reliability, making the 'fiction' description inaccurate."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Reframe the preference as a sociological convention within the scientific community. The mechanism is 'agreed-upon fiction': the community decides to act *as if* the best-corroborated theory is reliable to facilitate research and engineering, while privately maintaining the philosophical stance that it is unproven. This separates the 'context of action' (governed by convention) from the 'context of justification' (governed by strict falsification).","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.3},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference is indefensible on strict Popperian terms, revealing a fundamental fracture in his system: one can be a consistent Popperian in the lab (falsifying), but not in the world (acting), forcing a schism between the philosopher and the citizen.","counterconditions":["If Popper successfully unified these domains in his later works.","If 'acting' can be redefined to fit within System A.","If the schism is acceptable to working scientists."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Embrace the contradiction as a feature, not a bug. The mechanism is 'compartmentalization': the agent maintains two disjoint operating systems. System A (Science) rejects induction and probability. System B (Life/Action) unavoidably uses induction to survive. The 'preference' is the awkward handover point where System A hands control to System B, acknowledging that System A has nothing to say about the future. The cost is the integrity of a unified worldview.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.2}],"simulation_proposals":[]}